\par
# AgileFlow Architecture\par
\par
AgileFlow is a layered monolithic application. The API, domain model, business services, repositories, and React frontend live in one repository, but responsibilities are separated by project and feature boundaries.\par
\par
## Backend Architecture\par
\par
The backend uses .NET 8, ASP.NET Core, Entity Framework Core, ASP.NET Identity, JWT authentication, and SQL Server.\par
\par
```text\par
API -> Application -> Domain\par
API -> Infrastructure -> Application -> Domain\par
```\par
\par
- `backend/API`: controllers, middleware, dependency injection, authentication setup, CORS, Swagger, startup migrations, static upload serving, and hosted worker registration.\par
- `backend/Application`: DTOs, service interfaces, repository interfaces, AutoMapper profiles, and contracts shared by API and Infrastructure.\par
- `backend/Domain`: entities and enums such as workspace roles, sprint status, task status, task priority, approval status, commit status, and email event types.\par
- `backend/Infrastructure`: EF Core persistence, migrations, repository implementations, business services, SMTP email delivery, notification audit logging, and due-date reminder background work.\par
\par
The API layer should stay thin. Business rules belong in services, especially:\par
\par
- `WorkspaceService` and `WorkspaceAuthorizationService` for workspace membership and role rules.\par
- `ProjectService`, `SprintService`, and `TaskService` for delivery workflow rules.\par
- `BoardService` for board and column rules.\par
- `AuthService` and email services for verification, token, and notification workflows.\par
\par
## Frontend Architecture\par
\par
The frontend is a React 19 + TypeScript + Vite application using React Router v7, TanStack Query, Axios, React Hook Form, Zod, Tailwind, dnd-kit, Recharts, Framer Motion, Lucide icons, and shadcn-style UI primitives.\par
\par
Feature code is organized under `frontend/src/features`:\par
\par
- `auth`: login, register, refresh persistence, email verification, resend confirmation.\par
- `workspace`: workspace list/details, member table, role-based controls.\par
- `projects`: project list/details, project forms, sprint entry points.\par
- `sprints`: sprint details, progress chart, start/complete actions, embedded board.\par
- `board`: Kanban columns, task cards, column ordering, task movement.\par
- `tasks`: task detail modal, edit, submit/review, assignees, dependencies, activity logs.\par
- `account`: current-user profile and profile-picture upload.\par
\par
Frontend role checks only control visibility and ergonomics. Backend authorization remains the source of truth.\par
\par
## Authorization Model\par
\par
Workspace role controls all inner workspace operations:\par
\par
| Capability | Admin | TeamLead | Developer |\par
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\par
| Add/remove members | Yes | No | No |\par
| Change member roles | Yes | No | No |\par
| Edit workspace details | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Project CRUD | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Sprint CRUD/start/complete | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Board column CRUD/order | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Task create/delete/move/status | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Assign/unassign task users | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Add/remove task dependencies | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Approve/reject task review | Yes | Yes | No |\par
| Edit assigned task fields | Yes, if assigned or manager | Yes, if assigned or manager | Yes, if assigned |\par
| Submit assigned task for review | Yes, if assigned | Yes, if assigned | Yes, if assigned |\par
\par
The workspace creator remains an Admin and cannot be demoted or removed.\par
\par
## Task Lifecycle\par
\par
Tasks combine status, approval, commits, dependencies, and activity logs into one workflow:\par
\par
1. Admin or TeamLead creates and assigns tasks.\par
2. Assigned users can edit allowed fields and submit with a commit hash.\par
3. Submit sets approval to `Pending` and creates a pending commit record.\par
4. Admin or TeamLead reviews with a required comment.\par
5. Approval marks the commit as merged and the task as `Done` only when dependencies are also `Done` and `Approved`.\par
6. Rejection records the comment, marks the commit rejected, and keeps task data and assignees intact.\par
7. Sprint completion is blocked until every task is `Done` and `Approved`.\par
\par
## Email and Notifications\par
\par
Email delivery is isolated behind application interfaces and implemented in Infrastructure with SMTP.\par
\par
- Registration sends an email confirmation link and blocks login until confirmation.\par
- Workspace membership, task assignment, task submission, review decisions, and due-date reminders can send emails.\par
- `EmailNotificationLogs` stores audit and deduplication data.\par
- Email failures are logged and audited but do not roll back the original business action.\par
\par
## Persistence and Startup\par
\par
EF Core migrations define schema changes. The API applies migrations on startup using `Database.Migrate()`, so local development can create or update the database automatically when the configured SQL Server is available.\par
\par
Static profile-picture uploads are stored under `backend/API/wwwroot/uploads/profile-pictures/` and served by the API static-file middleware.\par
